This guide explains how candidates should approach the assignment from absolute basics to a working prototype, including environment setup, database creation, backend development, frontend development, real-time communication, testing, documentation, and final submission.

The objective is to help a beginner understand what to build, why it is required, how to build each component, how different technologies connect, and what to demonstrate during evaluation.

# 1. Understand the business problem first

## 1.1 What exactly are we building?

Imagine a software company called ABC Technologies.

It has three branches:

- Hyderabad
- Bangalore
- Chennai

Each branch has different employees and different projects.

The company wants all its employees to manage their work using a collaborative task management application.

For example:

- Hyderabad employees work on Project A.
- Bangalore employees work on Project B.
- Chennai employees work on Project C.

Employees should be able to create tasks, assign tasks, move tasks between columns, update task descriptions, and collaborate with other team members.

When one employee moves a task from "To Do" to "In Progress", other employees viewing the same board should immediately see that change.

However, Hyderabad employees must not be able to access Bangalore's private tasks.

This is the central problem the assignment wants candidates to solve.

